

Common data transformation steps (bronze → silver in a data warehouse)

1. Removing duplicates
Identifying records that appear more than once (e.g., same customer ID with multiple entries) and keeping only the most relevant version — usually the most recent one.

2. Cleaning text values
Stripping extra spaces, fixing inconsistent casing, removing stray characters that come from messy source systems or CSV exports.

3. Standardizing/normalizing values
Making sure the same concept is always represented the same way — e.g., gender stored as 'M', 'm', 'Male' all become one consistent value like 'Male'.

4. Handling missing data (NULLs)
Deciding what to do when a field is empty — replace with a default value like 'Unknown', leave as NULL intentionally, or flag the row for review.

5. Data type conversion
Converting raw text/strings into proper types — numbers stored as text become actual numbers, date-like strings become real date types. Often done "safely" so bad data doesn't crash the whole load, just gets flagged as NULL instead.

6. Deriving new columns
Calculating new values from existing ones — e.g., total sales from quantity × price, or age from a birthdate — values that don't exist raw in the source but are useful downstream.

7. Splitting one column into multiple
When a source system crams multiple pieces of information into a single field (like a product code that secretly contains a category + subcategory), breaking it apart into separate, usable columns.

8. Merging/concatenating columns
The reverse — combining separate fields (like first name + last name) into one usable field (full name).

9. Filtering out invalid or garbage rows
Removing rows with placeholder/nonsense values that source systems sometimes generate — impossible dates, negative quantities, obviously broken records.

10. Date parsing and reformatting
Converting inconsistent date formats (numbers, different string patterns) into a single standard date type, and validating that the date actually makes sense.

11. Mapping short codes to readable values
Source systems often store short internal codes (like 'CO', 'AC') — transformation translates these into human-readable categories for reporting.

12. Reconciling inconsistent location/country naming
Same country written multiple different ways across systems ('US', 'USA', 'United States') gets standardized to one canonical value.

13. Historical tracking (Slowly Changing Dimensions)
Deciding whether to simply overwrite a changed value, or keep a history of what it used to be — relevant when you want to track things like "this customer used to live in a different country."

14. Restructuring data shape (pivoting/unpivoting)
Turning rows into columns (or vice versa) — e.g., transforming yearly sales figures that were stored as separate rows into one row per customer with a column per year.

15. Business rule validation
Applying domain-specific rules — e.g., a transaction amount can't be negative, an account status must be one of a fixed set of allowed values — and flagging or excluding rows that break these rules.
